      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "other"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Narrows Bridge spans Greers Ferry Lake’s narrowest point between Higden and Greers Ferry, a key landmark noted by the Arkansas Department of Transportation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/higden_arkansas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In the early 1900s, Higden moved about a mile northeast to sit beside the Missouri and North Arkansas Railroad, seeking economic advantage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/higden_arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Higden’s sandy soils, part of the Salt Springs Barrens, historically supported pine forests, the only trees thriving on the thin, infertile ground.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/higden_arkansas",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Higden’s economy centers on Greers Ferry Lake tourism, with marinas and water‑sports facilities drawing seasonal visitors to the lake’s northern shore.",
